  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nRain is moving out of the forecast area early this morning, leaving \npartly cloudy skies in their wake. In areas that clear out this \nmorning, the radiative cooling, light and variable winds, and the \nrecent rainfall may all contribute to the potential for some pockets \nof patchy fog to develop this morning. However confidence is low at \nthis time, and model guidance does not show much in the way of \nchances for significant visibility reductions. \n\nBroad upper-level ridge will remain in place for the next couple of \ndays, continuing to bring above normal temperatures (highs in the \nupper 80s to 90s) today and Wednesday. Main forecast concern over \nthe period will be smoke coming in from the west. Latest high-\nresolution guidance brings smoke over most of the forecast area \ntoday and Wednesday, with the highest concentrations of vertically \nintegrated smoke over north central South Dakota. In terms of near-\nsurface smoke, concentrations are expected to be highest this \nmorning, potentially pushing air quality to Moderate levels (meaning \nthat mainly those sensitive to smoke would be affected).Through the \nrest of the day today and into Wednesday, near-surface smoke is \ngenerally expected to slowly decrease. \n\nThere is also some signal for some rain shower development over \nparts of northeastern South Dakota tonight. Inter-model consistency \non timing and location isn't high, but generally if storms pop up \nthey would come in the late afternoon to evening. Not much in the \nway of a severe threat is anticipated, but can't entirely rule out \nthe possibility of a stronger storm managing to produce marginal \nhail or wind this evening. Either way, storms will remain isolated \nto scattered and track southerly to southeasterly across eastern \nSouth Dakota. \n\nThe next chance for rain begins Wednesday night and continues into \nThursday. Rain will be possible throughout the day, with the \nheaviest rainfall and highest potential for storms coming in the \nlate afternoon to evening Thursday. However, some of the early \nshowers may help to limit the potential for storm development later. \nConfidence in the severe threat is fairly low, mainly due to the \nlack of shear in place. However, some pockets of up to 30 knots of 0-\n6km shear may be possible, which when coupled with MUCAPE values of \nroughly 1500-2000 J/kg may be enough to support initial development \nof storms. This is some signal for severe wind potential, as DCAPE \nvalues over 1000 J/kg and strong low-level lapse rates upwards of 8-\n9 C/km will be possible. Otherwise, not anticipating much in terms \nof the hail or tornado threat with storms on Thursday as it stands \nnow. At this time, a wind-driven Marginal Risk for severe weather is \nin place to cover storms Wednesday night over parts of Corson and \nDewey counties. Another Marginal Risk covers the entire CWA Thursday \nas well.\n\nLooking into the weekend, the upper-level ridge overhead will tilt \nto become positively tilted. A warm airmass will remain, supporting \ntemperatures roughly 5-10 degrees above normal. Dew points will also \nlower slightly over the weekend, from the upper 60s/low 70s mid-week \nto the low 60s by Sunday. The pattern will not be very conducive to \nrain chances, so expect dry conditions over the weekend.",
